# Go testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to the standard
`testing` package and its modern (1.23-1.25) additions. Applies only to
`*.go` files.

## Layout and naming

- Tests live beside the code as `<file>_test.go` in the same package (or
  `<pkg>_test` for black-box tests of the exported API); no separate `tests/`
  tree.
- Table-driven tests with `t.Run(tc.name, ...)` for more than one case; name
  cases descriptively, not `case1`/`case2`.
- `testdata/` for golden files and fixtures; no other fixture directory
  convention.

## Test helpers and lifecycle

- `t.Helper()` as the first line of a helper.
- `t.Cleanup` for teardown instead of a scattered `defer` chain.
- `t.Context()` (Go 1.24+) instead of hand-rolled `context.WithCancel`.
- `t.Parallel()` only when the subtest's fixtures are independent.

## Assertions and determinism

- Plain `if got != want { t.Errorf(...) }` or the project's configured
  assertion library; do not add a new one.
- Never `time.Sleep` to wait for async results; use a channel,
  `sync.WaitGroup`, `context` deadline, or a polling helper with a timeout.
- `httptest.NewServer`/`httptest.NewRecorder` for HTTP handler tests.

## Fuzzing and benchmarks

- `func FuzzX(f *testing.F)` with `f.Add(...)` seeds for parsers, decoders,
  and untrusted input formats; run `go test -fuzz=FuzzX` locally before
  relying on it in CI.
- Benchmarks use `for b.Loop() { ... }` (Go 1.24+), not
  `for i := 0; i < b.N; i++`.

## Race detection and coverage

- `go test -race ./...` for any change touching goroutines, shared state, or
  channels.
- New behavior gets a new test in the same change; a bug fix gets a
  regression test that fails before the fix.
